2026-10-17T00:00:00Z (RFD66606-2321) 'the autobattler fields Frames that wear
 # uuid: d7f6901b-4118-5006-8447-f81959dfc5eb
 # tags: rfd:title
 RFD:Committed  1 RFD
 Serials:VSekaiFabric

2026-10-17T00:00:01Z (RFD66606-2321) 'each fighter in the SolidCraft autobattler is a Frame put together from guest solids, rolled on survey tables, and it carries wear from round to round
 # tags: rfd:feature, rfd:no-final-newline
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:02Z (RFD66606-2321) 'the SolidCraft autobattler: its game script, its page and its zone version
 # tags: rfd:scope, rfd:no-final-newline
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:03Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Autobattler fighters are Frames that carry wear from round to round.
 # tags: rfd:hook
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:04Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Each unit was one bare solid with no identity, and every round started from zero. Nothing on screen said which unit was hurt or why a round ended.
 # tags: rfd:intrigue
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:05Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Run the game script with `--selftest` and `--rounds=N` to read a sim of the rounds.
 # tags: rfd:exit
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:06Z (RFD66606-2321) 'the autobattler fields Frames that wear
 # tags: rfd:details-title, rfd:no-final-newline
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:07Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Each squad in the SolidCraft autobattler fields five Frames. A Frame's body is put together from solids the guest kernel builds, and its species, presentation and age come from three d100 rolls on the population survey tables of our appendix-E dataset. A Frame that survives a round stays with wear and leaves after five rounds, and each side earns scrip per round, as in the Gyre setting. The operator asked for more Frames, game feel, the Gyre flavour and readability on 2026-10-17.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:08Z (RFD66606-2321) '- RFD66606-2379, where the desktop builds ship: the release that carries this game.
 # tags: rfd:related
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:09Z (RFD66606-2321) '- RFD66606-2085, RFD66606-2091 and RFD66606-2120, the Gyre setting (abandoned): the source of Frames, wear and scrip.
 # tags: rfd:related
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:10Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Roles, props and the zone as requested
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:11Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Each Frame also has one of three roles, melee, range or cyber, that beat each other as in rock, paper, scissors. The operator requested the roles on 2026-10-18. Each role and the plant also need a prop in a mirrored arena: cover, a relay per side or soil. The operator requested them the same day. On 2026-10-19 the operator requested that the game also run as a fabric zone that headsets join, with the store kept off every frame and every tick.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:12Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Sources
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:13Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Drafted on 2026-10-17 from the game script as shipped, its self-test logs on each system and the operator's requests of the same day.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:14Z (RFD66606-2321) 'What a Frame is
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:15Z (RFD66606-2321) 'The guest builds 18 solids while the game loads: 12 whole solids and six parts (a head, a limb, a torso, a ring, a cube and a base). A Frame places parts by species and merges them into one mesh, so a Frame is one draw call. The parts become separate pieces only when it breaks. The bounds of the parts set health, damage, speed and reach, as the single solid did before.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:16Z (RFD66606-2321) '- Species: humanoid 1-50, semi-humanoid 51-88, synthetic 89-94, uplifted animal 95-96, plant 97-98, exotic 99-100.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:17Z (RFD66606-2321) '- Presentation: feminine 1-71, masculine 72-89, other 90-100.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:18Z (RFD66606-2321) '- Age: youth 1-58, juvenile 59-75, adult 76-87, no age 88-99, infant 100.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:19Z (RFD66606-2321) 'The bands are the raw survey distribution, tables E.8 to E.10. Age scales the body and the head. Each species but humanoid adds one trait: semi-humanoid is fast, uplifted animal is very fast with less health, synthetic is plated and slower, plant regrows and is slow, and exotic is one bare whole solid that hits hard.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:20Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Wear and scrip
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:21Z (RFD66606-2321) 'A Frame that survives a round gains one point of wear, and its maximum health drops by a tenth of its base for each point. At five points it leaves. New drafts fill the empty places. Each side earns two scrip per round plus one per Frame left, and the winner three more.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:22Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Feel and readability
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:23Z (RFD66606-2321) 'A hit flashes the Frame and plays a short generated sound. A Frame that breaks throws its parts and shakes the camera. Each Frame has a health bar and a name tag, a little of its side's colour shows even in shade, and the end of a round shows the Frames left and the scrip earned.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:24Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Roles
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:25Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Melee beats range, range beats cyber and cyber beats melee. A hit on the role the attacker beats does 1.6 times its damage, a hit on the role that beats it does 0.6 times, and a hit on the same role does its normal damage. Reach puts the roles in order: melee hits at the reach of its body, cyber at 3.8 m and range at 7 m, so a range Frame shoots a cyber Frame before the cyber Frame can answer, and a melee Frame has to close the gap to a range Frame.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:26Z (RFD66606-2321) '- Melee: 1.25 times the health, 1.15 times the speed, an attack every 0.8 s, and a shield in front. Its hits push the target back.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:27Z (RFD66606-2321) '- Range: 0.75 times the health, 0.8 times the damage, 0.9 times the speed, an attack every 1.1 s, and a barrel at the shoulder. Each shot is a small sphere that flies to the target.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:28Z (RFD66606-2321) '- Cyber: 0.95 times the health, 0.7 times the damage, an attack every 1.0 s, and a dish on top. Each hit adds 0.7 s to the target's next attack and shows a ring that spreads over the target.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:29Z (RFD66606-2321) 'The role's parts come from the same catalog of guest solids and merge into the Frame's one mesh, but they do not count toward its health, damage or reach. Each squad's draft holds every role at least once, so every round shows the triangle. The other two places draw a role at random from the round's seed. The name tag shows the role before the species.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:30Z (RFD66606-2321) 'What the world gives each role
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:31Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Each role and the plant now need something in the arena, so a sim shows what the world adds and what it takes away. The operator requested these props on 2026-10-18, for every role at once so that no role is the only one that depends on the world.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:32Z (RFD66606-2321) '- Range needs a clear line. Five cover blocks stand in the middle. A range Frame does not shoot through cover. It walks around the block until the line is clear. Every Frame walks around cover, not through it.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:33Z (RFD66606-2321) '- Cyber needs its relay. Each side has a relay mast, with its reach drawn as a faint disc on the floor. A cyber Frame jams only within 6.5 m of its own standing relay; away from it, the hit does half damage and no jam. A cyber Frame waits at the edge of the disc while friends who are not cyber still fight. Any Frame can attack the enemy relay (160 health). A relay that goes down stays down until the next round.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:34Z (RFD66606-2321) '- Melee needs open ground. Within 0.9 m of cover a melee Frame moves at 0.6 times its speed and its hits do not push the target back.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:35Z (RFD66606-2321) '- A plant needs soil. It regrows (4 health per second) only on one of four soil patches. Below half health it walks to the nearest patch and stays until it is nearly whole.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:36Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Equity is a property of the layout: each prop has a twin of the same shape at the mirror place across the middle line, side 0's relay is on side 0's half, and a tie in which way to walk round cover goes the same way for both sides. The self-test checks the mirror and fails when one block is moved 0.5 m (the control). It also checks that a line through the middle block is blocked and a line past the blocks is clear. Each round prints a `WORLD` line: seconds range Frames spent with no clear line, cyber hits with and without the relay, seconds melee Frames spent beside cover, seconds plants spent on soil, and each relay's health. `--rounds=N` with `--selftest` runs N rounds for a longer sim.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:37Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Zone server and VR mode
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:38Z (RFD66606-2321) 'The round is deterministic: one seed gives the same `ROUND`, `DAMAGE` and `WORLD` lines on the web and on the three desktop systems. That makes the game a fit for an authoritative zone. The zone server runs the round headless, and every client shows the same round.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:39Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Three loops run at three rates, and only the last touches the store.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:40Z (RFD66606-2321) '- The headset renders at 144 Hz, a frame every 6.9 ms. It predicts poses locally and never waits on the network or on a commit.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:41Z (RFD66606-2321) '- The zone runs the round at a fixed tick. At the start of a round it sends the seed and both drafts on a reliable stream. Each tick it sends every Frame's place and health as datagrams, where the next tick replaces a lost one, and hits, breaks and the round's end on the reliable stream. The zone and transport are those of &{repo("interactor-fabric-zone")}.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:42Z (RFD66606-2321) '- After a tick, the zone hands its SQLite journal image to a save thread if it changed. The save thread writes the image to SQLite on FoundationDB (&{repo("datasource-store")}) and keeps only the newest one: a save that is still in flight when the next is ready is replaced, not queued. A slow commit makes the stored image older. It never holds a tick or a frame.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:43Z (RFD66606-2321) 'The store's place in the third loop comes from a measurement. On 2026-10-19, sixteen players committing one row 20 times a second waited 5.5 to 6.0 ms for the median commit on the Windows desk's FoundationDB under WSL, and over one player-day the worst waits reached 3.3 to 4.7 s. A typical commit is already a whole frame at 144 Hz, and the worst is hundreds of frames.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:44Z (RFD66606-2321) 'The zone version lives in a new interactor repository, named interactor-solidcraft-zone and placed in the goal manifest when it is made. The game's Godot project moves there as the one source, with a zone-server mode and a headset mode. The Space keeps the web build and the downloads, built from that repository. The guest kernel and the zone repository stay as they are and are used as dependencies.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:45Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Each step has a check, and each check has a control.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:46Z (RFD66606-2321) '- The Space's web build prints the same lines after the move as before it.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:47Z (RFD66606-2321) '- Two headless clients beside a zone server print the server's `ROUND`, `DAMAGE` and `WORLD` lines. A client given another seed must differ.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:48Z (RFD66606-2321) '- The headset mode runs under the XR Pilot desktop runtime, which an agent drives with no person in a headset.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:49Z (RFD66606-2321) '- The save thread reports the age of the newest stored image over a player-day: its worst and how often it passes 1, 2 and 5 s. The planted slow commit must raise it.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:50Z (RFD66606-2321) 'The zone simulates at 60 Hz and sends rows under RFD66606-2326's byte budget. Two choices are open: whether the headset client is the V-Sekai client or a scene in the game's own project, and which host runs FoundationDB in production. A run on a plain Linux runner, pending on 2026-10-19, says whether the multi-second waits come from the WSL host.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:51Z (RFD66606-2321) 'The autobattler is the network test bench
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:52Z (RFD66606-2321) 'RFD66606-2326's numbers so far come from walkers generated for the test, whose rows never repeat. The autobattler gives real traffic with an answer key. One seed fixes every round, so the server's replay of a seed is the truth for every row a client receives, at every tick.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:53Z (RFD66606-2321) '- Scale. One zone runs many arenas side by side, from one to a few hundred, each a round of ten Frames on its own seed, with headless players placed so their view holds from tens to thousands of Frames. Arenas start a few ticks apart, so hits, breaks and round ends spread over time.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:54Z (RFD66606-2321) '- Classes. The zone carries what the game carries, not one kind of mover: Frames on scripted moves, players' tracked heads and hands, props at rest that change only when touched, and crowds of pedestrians who stop, start and walk in pairs. Headless players replay recorded head and hand tracks, since tracked motion has sensor jitter a script lacks. Every report is split by class.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:55Z (RFD66606-2321) '- Scenes. Crowds come as the scenes people make: a shopping street, a standing concert, a seated stadium with a wave, a queue, a scramble crossing, a dance floor, a parade, a festival plaza, a theatre, a picnic lawn, an exit through one door, and a ring of players talking. Each scene's density and pace come from footage counted the way the blue-hour street walk was, and in social scenes most of the crowd is tracked players.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:56Z (RFD66606-2321) '- Oracle. Each headless client decodes its rows, moves Frames along their sent velocity between rows, and compares what it holds with the server's replay of the same seeds. It reports the worst position error and how long a Frame stayed wrong. A client must never hold a Frame the replay says has left, or a value that was never sent.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:57Z (RFD66606-2321) '- Link. Between server and clients sits a relay that drops and delays datagrams: no loss, 1% and 5%, at 50, 100 and 200 ms of round trip.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:58Z (RFD66606-2321) '- Record. The relay writes every message it passes. The recordings are the corpus for the static zstd dictionary: dictionaries built from them at 1, 8, 64, 256 and 1024 MB are measured on held-out recordings in bytes per row, and the size stops growing where the gain flattens.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:00:59Z (RFD66606-2321) '- Report. Bytes per player per second against the budget, bytes per row for plain zstd, for patch-from against the last row the client acknowledged, and for each dictionary size, and the oracle's errors. Saves go through the store's latest-wins thread and report save age.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:01:00Z (RFD66606-2321) 'On rows generated for the test, sent as the gap from the client's own prediction, props at rest cost 0.31 bytes a row, pedestrians 2.16 and tracked points 3.96. Walkers that never stop cost 0.47, which flatters prediction, so the bench uses pedestrians and never zombies. Scripted scenes flatter it the same way: a scripted queue, wave or crossing cost 0.31 to 0.34, the price of a prop at rest, and a scripted concert 2.04. Scenes are replayed from recorded motion where a licence allows, and a scene full of players costs what their tracked heads and hands cost. The budget gives players near each other the first share, and the bench sets the split from recorded traffic.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:01:01Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Each measurement has a planted defect that must show in the report:
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:01:02Z (RFD66606-2321) '- the budget ignored, which must push bytes per second over it;
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:01:03Z (RFD66606-2321) '- a client holding another dictionary version, which must fail to decode and never decode wrong values;
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:01:04Z (RFD66606-2321) '- the clock counter per row, which must make a client order rows wrong past 256 rows in a tick;
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:01:05Z (RFD66606-2321) '- the velocity scale read per tick, which must raise position error;
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:01:06Z (RFD66606-2321) '- a dropped leave message, which must leave a Frame the replay has removed;
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:01:07Z (RFD66606-2321) '- the store's slow commit, which must raise save age and nothing else.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:01:08Z (RFD66606-2321) 'What was measured
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:01:09Z (RFD66606-2321) 'The change is in the game script only, so the guest and every translation hash stayed valid. On 2026-10-17 the web build and the macOS, Windows and Linux zips each started translated, built 18 solids and printed `RESULT PASS`. The script cost per physics frame rose from about 0.23 ms to about 0.3 ms on the Mac. The web game's slowest frame in the first round read 139 to 145 ms before and after. Native frame times on the Mac were not compared: other jobs on the same machine made them vary more than the change.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:01:10Z (RFD66606-2321) 'On 2026-10-18 the roles changed the game script only, again. The self-test first checks the triangle: every role beats exactly one other and loses to exactly one. A control table in which melee loses to both others must fail the same check, and it does. The macOS, Windows and Linux zips and the web build in a desktop browser then printed the same two rounds, with the same winners and the same damage per pair of roles, and `RESULT PASS`. In round 1, range did 303 damage to cyber and cyber did 57 to range. The web game's slowest frame in round 1 read 150 ms with the roles and 134 ms without them, one run each in the same browser, so the difference is not measured past noise. The release kept its tag, because the guest did not change. Its zips and SHA256SUMS were replaced.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:01:11Z (RFD66606-2321) 'On 2026-10-18 the world props changed the game script only, a third time. The self-test printed `WORLD mirror=true control=false line_through=false line_beside=true` on each system. The macOS, Windows and Linux zips and the web build in a desktop browser printed the same `ROUND`, `DAMAGE` and `WORLD` lines for the four rounds they share. The web run went on to nine rounds and the macOS run to twelve. Over the twelve rounds each side won six. Per round, range Frames spent 0.3 to 3.0 s with no clear line, melee Frames spent 1.2 to 9.0 s beside cover, and cyber Frames landed 1 to 17 hits with the relay and 0 to 13 without it. Plants stood on soil in four of the twelve rounds, for up to 8.0 s. Plants are 2 in 100 of the species rolls, so a sim that wants to read the soil rule needs many rounds. No relay went down in the twelve rounds (the lowest read 103 of 160). The first version moved a blocked range Frame sideways only, and in one round two range Frames stayed blocked for 34 s until the 45 s limit ended it. A blocked range Frame now walks around the block, and no round of the twelve reached the limit.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:01:12Z (RFD66606-2321) 'The problem
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-10-17T00:01:13Z (RFD66606-2321) 'Each unit was one solid: a box, a cylinder, a sphere or a torus.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD2321  1 LINE
 Text:Written
